  "stemBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "A graph shows a straight line L passing through A(−2, −1), B(0, 3) and C(3, 9). (a) Calculate the gradient of L using TWO labelled points. (b) State the y-intercept. (c) Determine the equation of L. (d) State the x-intercept."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Think first: Read the coordinates of the labelled points from the grid first — everything else follows from them."
    },
    {
      "type": "asset",
      "storagePath": "assets/question_visuals/p122_q06.jpg",
      "altText": "Source visual for edmar-csec-p122-q06 from workbook page 122."
    }
  ],
  "solutionSteps": [
    {
      "stepNo": 1,
      "instruction": "Read the coordinates of the labelled points from the graph.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "A on the x-axis side at (−2, −1), B on the y-axis at (0, 3), C at (3, 9)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: A(−2, −1), B(0, 3), C(3, 9)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 2,
      "instruction": "Use B and C in the gradient formula.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "m = (9 − 3)/(3 − 0) = 6/3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: m = 2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 3,
      "instruction": "Confirm with A and B.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "m = (3 − (−1))/(0 − (−2)) = 4/2 = 2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: Gradient confirmed as 2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 4,
      "instruction": "State the y-intercept — where the line crosses the y-axis.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "The line passes through B(0, 3)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: y-intercept = 3, i.e. (0, 3)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 5,
      "instruction": "Write the equation using y = mx + c.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "m = 2 and c = 3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: y = 2x + 3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 6,
      "instruction": "Find the x-intercept by setting y = 0.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "0 = 2x + 3, so 2x = −3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: x = −1.5, i.e. (−1.5, 0)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "strategyBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Read A, B and C from the graph, use two of them in the gradient formula, take c from where the line crosses the y-axis, then set y = 0 to find the x-intercept."
    }
  ],
  "finalAnswerBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "(a) Gradient = 2  (b) y-intercept = 3, at (0, 3)  (c) y = 2x + 3  (d) x-intercept = −1.5, at (−1.5, 0)"
    }
  ],
  "whyThisWorks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "A straight line has the same gradient between any two of its points, so choosing A and B or B and C must give the same value — that is a built-in check. In y = mx + c, m is that gradient and c is the y-value where the line meets the x = 0 axis, so once both are read off the equation is complete. The x-intercept is simply the value of x that makes y zero."
    }
  ],
